                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding how a sailor navigates to the shore. Can you help me work through this multiple-choice question?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through each option and see if it makes sense. First, we have option A: closing the sails quickly. What do you think about this option?\n\nStudent: Well, I think closing the sails would slow down the boat, but I'm not sure if that would help the sailor navigate to the shore.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Closing the sails would indeed slow down the boat, but it wouldn't necessarily help the sailor navigate towards the shore. Let's move on to option B: setting out to sea. What are your thoughts on this one?\n\nStudent: That doesn't seem right to me. If the sailor wants to get to the shore, going further out to sea would be the opposite of what they want to do.\n\nProfessor: Exactly! Setting out to sea would take the sailor further away from their destination. Now, let's consider option C: making an adjustment to the rudder. How does this option sound to you?\n\nStudent: That sounds like it could be the right answer. Adjusting the rudder would change the direction of the boat, which could help the sailor navigate to the shore.\n\nProfessor: You're on the right track! Making an adjustment to the rudder would indeed change the direction of the boat, allowing the sailor to steer towards the shore. Finally, let's look at option D: taking the afternoon off. What do you think about this one?\n\nStudent: That doesn't seem like it would help the sailor get to the shore at all. If anything, it would just delay their progress.\n\nProfessor: You're absolutely right. Taking the afternoon off wouldn't help the sailor navigate to the shore. So, based on our discussion, which option do you think is the correct answer?\n\nStudent: I think the correct answer is C) making an adjustment to the rudder.\n\nProfessor: Great job! You've worked through the question step by step and arrived at the correct answer. Keep using this approach, and you'll continue to improve your problem-solving skills.",
