                    {
                        "id": "a8f6a4b9-9d17-47d1-9e14-59eaeb63b8c1",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Define deserts\nDeserts are regions that receive very little rainfall, typically less than 250 millimeters per year.\n\nStep 2: Understand the relationship between deserts and sunshine\nSunshine is an important factor in the formation of deserts. The lack of cloud cover in deserts allows for more direct sunlight to reach the ground, which leads to higher temperatures and evaporation rates. This, in turn, leads to a lack of moisture in the air and on the ground, which is a defining characteristic of deserts.\n\nStep 3: Analyze the answer choices\nA) they get low sunlight - This contradicts the relationship between deserts and sunshine. Therefore, this answer choice is incorrect.\nB) deserts get surplus sun - This answer choice is also incorrect as it implies that deserts receive more sunlight than they need, which is not the case.\nC) deserts get little sun - This answer choice is correct. Deserts receive little sun due to the lack of cloud cover and high temperatures that result from direct sunlight.\nD) deserts are always cloudy - This answer choice is incorrect as it contradicts the relationship between deserts and sunshine.\n\nStep 4: Conclusion\nDeserts are characterized by little sun due to the lack of cloud cover and high temperatures that result from direct sunlight. Therefore, the correct answer is C) deserts get little sun.",
