                    {
                        "id": "98577819-f5ab-4b56-85ed-2200951d24c4",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-10",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the concept of echolocation. Echolocation is a method used by certain animals, such as bats and dolphins, to determine the location, distance, and characteristics of objects in their environment by emitting sounds and listening to the echoes that return.\n\nStep 2: Analyze each answer choice in relation to echolocation.\n\nA) Distance: Echolocation can determine the distance of an object based on the time it takes for the emitted sound to return as an echo. The longer it takes for the echo to return, the farther away the object is.\n\nB) Shape: Echolocation can provide information about an object's shape, as the returning echoes will vary depending on the surface and contours of the object. This allows the animal to create a mental image of the object's shape.\n\nC) Size: Echolocation can also provide information about an object's size, as larger objects will produce stronger echoes, while smaller objects will produce weaker echoes.\n\nD) Temperature: Echolocation relies on sound waves, which are not affected by temperature. Therefore, echolocation cannot detect an object's temperature.\n\nStep 3: Choose the answer that echolocation cannot detect.\n\nThe correct answer is D) temperature.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f852b78f-9acd-4df3-a24a-8b8369e803fa",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
