                    {
                        "id": "bdb60b3d-8fd3-46ab-b38f-96437779962e",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Echolocation is a biological sonar used by some animals, such as bats and dolphins, to navigate and locate objects in their environment. It involves emitting sounds and listening to the echoes that return after bouncing off objects. By analyzing the time delay and frequency of the returning echoes, animals can determine the distance, size, and shape of objects around them.\n\nThe original question is: Echolocation can't detect an object's\nA) distance\nB) shape\nC) size\nD) temperature\n\nTo answer this question, let's first establish what echolocation can detect:\n1) Can echolocation detect an object's distance?\n2) Can echolocation detect an object's shape?\n3) Can echolocation detect an object's size?\n\n1) Echolocation can detect an object's distance by measuring the time it takes for the emitted sound to return as an echo. The longer the time delay, the farther away the object is.\n\n2) Echolocation can detect an object's shape by analyzing the pattern of the returning echoes. Different shapes will cause the sound waves to bounce off and return in different patterns, allowing the animal to determine the shape of the object.\n\n3) Echolocation can detect an object's size by analyzing the intensity and frequency of the returning echoes. Larger objects will produce stronger echoes, while smaller objects will produce weaker echoes. Additionally, the frequency of the returning echoes can provide information about the size of the object, as different sized objects will absorb and reflect sound waves differently.\n\nNow that we have established that echolocation can detect distance, shape, and size, we can conclude that the answer to the original question is:\n\nEcholocation can't detect an object's\nD) temperature\n\nEcholocation relies on sound waves, which do not provide information about an object's temperature. To detect temperature, animals would need to rely on other senses, such as infrared sensing or direct contact.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1f9123a4-6df6-4d0a-9ee6-0abdd3ab12aa",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) temperature.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/13 04:04:15",
