                    {
                        "id": "f922c992-e1ef-461d-87ac-b7e2672f3b6f",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding how echolocation works and what it can detect. Can you help me with this multiple-choice question?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Echolocation is a method used by some animals, like bats and dolphins, to determine the location and characteristics of objects in their environment by emitting sounds and listening to the echoes. Now, let's look at the options one by one.\n\nStudent: Okay, so the first option is \"distance.\" Can echolocation detect an object's distance?\n\nProfessor: Yes, it can. When an animal emits a sound, it travels through the air or water and bounces off objects, creating echoes. The time it takes for the echo to return to the animal can be used to calculate the distance to the object. The longer it takes for the echo to return, the farther away the object is.\n\nStudent: I see. What about the second option, \"shape\"? Can echolocation determine an object's shape?\n\nProfessor: Yes, it can. The way the sound waves bounce off an object and return to the animal can provide information about the object's shape. For example, if the sound waves bounce off a flat surface, the echo will be different than if they bounce off a curved surface. By analyzing the echoes, the animal can determine the shape of the object.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. Now, the third option is \"size.\" Can echolocation detect an object's size?\n\nProfessor: Yes, it can. The intensity of the echo can provide information about the size of the object. Larger objects will generally produce stronger echoes, while smaller objects will produce weaker echoes. By comparing the intensity of the echoes, the animal can determine the size of the object.\n\nStudent: Alright, so the last option is \"temperature.\" Can echolocation detect an object's temperature?\n\nProfessor: No, echolocation cannot directly detect an object's temperature. The sound waves used in echolocation are not sensitive to temperature differences. So, the correct answer to the question is D) temperature.\n\nStudent: I understand now. Echolocation can detect an object's distance, shape, and size, but not its temperature. Thank you for explaining it to me, Professor!\n\nProfessor: You're welcome! I'm glad I could help you understand echolocation better. If you have any more questions, don't hesitate to ask.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f3360e32-b5b2-4f3e-8cac-7e796940ebef",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) temperature.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
