                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider which of the given options could potentially lead to a decrease in the amount of brush in a park. We should also consider any additional factors that may be relevant to the situation.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Brush refers to shrubs, bushes, and small trees that grow in a park.\n- The growth and survival of brush can be affected by various factors such as weather conditions, human activities, and the presence of other organisms.\n\nSub-questions and answers:\n\n1) Can a dry season cause a decrease in the amount of brush in a park?\nYes, a dry season can cause a decrease in the amount of brush in a park. During a dry season, there is less water available for plants, which can lead to the death of some brush species and a decrease in their overall growth.\n\n2) Can a lot of rain cause a decrease in the amount of brush in a park?\nWhile excessive rain can sometimes lead to flooding or other issues that may negatively impact plant growth, it is generally not a direct cause of a decrease in the amount of brush in a park. In fact, rain can often promote the growth of plants, including brush.\n\n3) Can snakes sheltering under the brush cause a decrease in the amount of brush in a park?\nSnakes sheltering under the brush are unlikely to cause a significant decrease in the amount of brush in a park. While they may cause some minor disturbances to the brush, their presence alone would not be enough to cause a noticeable decrease in the overall amount of brush.\n\n4) Can people walking by the brush on trails cause a decrease in the amount of brush in a park?\nPeople walking by the brush on trails may cause some minor damage to the brush, but this is unlikely to be a significant cause of a decrease in the overall amount of brush in a park. However, if there is extensive human activity in the park, such as construction or landscaping, this could potentially lead to a decrease in the amount of brush.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, the most likely cause of the decrease in the amount of brush in the park is option A) the season has been quite dry.",
