                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand what Shivambu is and what its practitioners believe about ammonia.\n\nShivambu is an ancient Indian practice, also known as \"urine therapy,\" which involves the consumption of one's own urine for purported health benefits. It is based on the belief that urine has healing properties and can help treat various ailments.\n\nAmmonia is a compound of nitrogen and hydrogen, which is present in small amounts in urine. It is a waste product produced by the body during the breakdown of proteins. In high concentrations, ammonia can be toxic and harmful to the body.\n\nNow, let's generate sub-questions to help answer the original question:\n\n1. Do Shivambu practitioners believe that urine has healing properties?\n2. Are Shivambu practitioners aware of the presence of ammonia in urine?\n3. Do they believe that the ammonia in urine is harmful or beneficial?\n\n1. Yes, Shivambu practitioners believe that urine has healing properties and can help treat various ailments.\n\n2. It is likely that Shivambu practitioners are aware of the presence of ammonia in urine, as they believe in the therapeutic properties of urine and may have some knowledge about its composition.\n\n3. Since Shivambu practitioners believe in the healing properties of urine, it is likely that they do not consider the ammonia present in urine to be harmful. They may believe that the small amounts of ammonia in urine are not toxic and that the overall benefits of urine therapy outweigh any potential risks.\n\nBased on this reasoning, the answer to the original question is:\n\nB) False - Shivambu practitioners do not believe ammonia is unhealthy.",
