                    {
                        "id": "751c89bd-c6bc-4049-8477-fcbc08dd5934",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand the origin of the Qwerty keyboard layout and the history of computers.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- The Qwerty keyboard layout was created by Christopher Latham Sholes, an American newspaper editor, and printer.\n- The layout was designed for mechanical typewriters to reduce the likelihood of jamming by placing frequently used letter pairs farther apart.\n- The first commercially successful typewriter featuring the Qwerty layout was the Sholes and Glidden typewriter, also known as the Remington No. 1, which was produced in 1874.\n- The history of computers can be traced back to the invention of the abacus, but the first electronic digital computer, the Colossus, was built during World War II in 1943.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) When was the Qwerty keyboard layout invented?\nThe Qwerty keyboard layout was invented in the early 1870s by Christopher Latham Sholes.\n\n2) When were the first electronic digital computers invented?\nThe first electronic digital computer, the Colossus, was built in 1943.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nSince the Qwerty keyboard layout was invented in the early 1870s and the first electronic digital computer was built in 1943, the Qwerty keyboard layout predates computers.\n\nSo, the answer is A) True.",
                        "answers": [
