                    {
                        "id": "7102902c-e6eb-4d3c-b65d-0c5fbeaa0bd6",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I have a question about the Qwerty keyboard layout. Did it predate computers?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. The Qwerty keyboard layout was actually invented in the 19th century, long before computers were developed. Would you like me to explain the history behind it?\n\nStudent: Yes, please. How did the Qwerty layout come about?\n\nProfessor: The Qwerty layout was created by Christopher Latham Sholes, an American newspaper editor and printer. He invented the typewriter in 1868, and the Qwerty layout was designed specifically for this machine. The layout was created to reduce the likelihood of the typebars jamming when two adjacent keys were pressed in quick succession.\n\nStudent: So, the layout was designed for efficiency in typing?\n\nProfessor: Not exactly. The layout was actually designed to slow down typing to some extent, to prevent the typebars from jamming. However, over time, typists became accustomed to the layout and were able to type quickly and efficiently despite the initial intention.\n\nStudent: How did the Qwerty layout become the standard for keyboards?\n\nProfessor: The Qwerty layout became popular because it was used on the first commercially successful typewriter, the Sholes and Glidden typewriter, which was later renamed the Remington No. 1. As more typewriters were produced, the Qwerty layout became the standard, and it was eventually adopted for use on computer keyboards as well.\n\nStudent: I see. So, the Qwerty layout was created for typewriters and then carried over to computer keyboards. That means the correct answer to the question is A) True, right?\n\nProfessor: That's correct! The Qwerty keyboard layout did indeed predate computers. Good job on understanding the history behind it.",
