                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can spiders help eggplant farmers control parasites?\n\nProfessor: Yes, spiders can help eggplant farmers control parasites. So, the answer is A) True. Do you want me to explain how?\n\nStudent: Yes, please. How do spiders help in controlling parasites?\n\nProfessor: Spiders are natural predators of many insects, including those that can be harmful to eggplants. By having spiders in the fields, they can help reduce the population of these harmful insects, which in turn helps protect the eggplants from damage.\n\nStudent: What kind of insects do spiders prey on that are harmful to eggplants?\n\nProfessor: Some common pests that attack eggplants and are preyed upon by spiders include aphids, whiteflies, and various caterpillars. These insects can cause significant damage to eggplants by feeding on their leaves and fruits.\n\nStudent: How do farmers encourage spiders to be present in their fields?\n\nProfessor: Farmers can encourage spiders to be present in their fields by providing them with a suitable habitat. This can include planting cover crops, maintaining a diverse ecosystem, and avoiding the use of broad-spectrum insecticides that can harm spiders as well as the target pests.\n\nStudent: Are there any drawbacks to relying on spiders for pest control?\n\nProfessor: While spiders can be beneficial for controlling pests, they may not always be enough to keep pest populations in check. In some cases, additional pest control methods may be necessary, such as the use of targeted insecticides or the introduction of other beneficial insects.\n\nStudent: I see. So, spiders can help eggplant farmers control parasites, but they might not be the only solution needed for effective pest control.\n\nProfessor: Exactly! Spiders can be a valuable part of an integrated pest management strategy, but it's important for farmers to monitor pest populations and use additional control methods as needed to protect their crops.",
