                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Rainbows are formed by the refraction and reflection of sunlight through water droplets in the atmosphere. The colors of the rainbow are red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are yin and yang colors?\n2. Is there a scientific basis for the concept of yin and yang colors?\n3. Are rainbows devoid of color?\n\nAnswering sub-questions:\n1. Yin and yang are concepts from Chinese philosophy that represent opposite but complementary forces. Yin colors are cool, dark, and passive, while yang colors are warm, bright, and active. Examples of yin colors are black, blue, and green, while examples of yang colors are red, orange, and yellow.\n2. The concept of yin and yang colors is not recognized in modern science. It is a cultural belief that has no empirical evidence to support it.\n3. Rainbows are not devoid of color. They are actually made up of all the colors of the visible spectrum, which are separated by the refraction of light through water droplets. The colors of the rainbow are not yin or yang, but rather a continuous spectrum of colors.\n\nAnswer: B) False. Rainbows are not devoid of color, and there is no scientific basis for the concept of yin and yang colors.",
