                    {
                        "id": "e2c4be78-045b-4f0f-8646-759995043b66",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "B) False\n\nTo determine whether a beaver's teeth would rival that of a Smilodon, we need to compare the teeth of both animals.\n\nBeavers are rodents known for their large, sharp incisors that are used for gnawing on wood. These incisors continuously grow throughout the beaver's life, which is necessary because they are constantly being worn down by the hard materials they chew on. Beavers have a total of 20 teeth, including their incisors, premolars, and molars.\n\nSmilodon, also known as the saber-toothed cat, was a prehistoric carnivorous mammal that lived during the Pleistocene epoch. Smilodon is known for its long, curved canine teeth, which could grow up to 11 inches (28 centimeters) in length. These teeth were used for stabbing and slicing through the flesh of their prey. Smilodon had a total of 30 teeth, including their canines, incisors, premolars, and molars.\n\nComparing the teeth of beavers and Smilodon, we can see that they have different functions and structures. Beavers have large incisors for gnawing on wood, while Smilodon had long, curved canines for stabbing and slicing through flesh. Additionally, Smilodon's teeth were much larger than those of a beaver.\n\nTherefore, it is false to say that a beaver's teeth would rival that of a Smilodon, as their teeth are adapted for different purposes and have different structures.",
