                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me if it's safe to eat kidney?\n\nProfessor: Sure, let me break it down for you. When you say \"kidney,\" are you referring to the organ or the food?\n\nStudent: I'm referring to the food.\n\nProfessor: Okay, just wanted to clarify. The answer to your question is that it depends on the type of kidney you're talking about. If you're referring to beef or pork kidney, then it is safe to eat as long as it is cooked properly.\n\nStudent: How do you cook it properly?\n\nProfessor: You should cook it until the internal temperature reaches at least 160\u00b0F to kill any harmful bacteria. It's also important to make sure that the meat is not pink or raw in the middle.\n\nStudent: What about other types of kidney, like chicken or lamb?\n\nProfessor: Chicken and lamb kidneys are not commonly consumed in the United States, but they are eaten in other parts of the world. However, it's important to note that some types of kidney can contain high levels of toxins, so it's best to consult with a healthcare professional or a trusted source before consuming them.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that to me, Professor.",
