                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, let's first define what anatomical and symbolic hearts are.\n\nAn anatomical heart refers to the actual organ in the human body that pumps blood through the circulatory system. It is a muscular organ with a complex structure, consisting of four chambers (two atria and two ventricles), valves, and blood vessels.\n\nA symbolic heart, on the other hand, is a simplified representation of the heart that is often used to symbolize love, affection, and emotions. It is typically depicted as a symmetrical shape with two rounded lobes at the top and a pointed end at the bottom.\n\nNow, let's compare the two:\n\nAn anatomical heart is a complex, three-dimensional organ with a specific structure and function. It is not symmetrical and has a more irregular shape.\n\nA symbolic heart is a simple, two-dimensional shape that is symmetrical and does not resemble the actual structure of the heart.\n\nBased on this comparison, we can conclude that anatomical and symbolic hearts look remarkably different.\n\nSo, the answer is:\n\nA) True",
