                    {
                        "id": "4f08bf88-2af9-4a54-ad94-e4333a846aa8",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I have a question about the difference between anatomical and symbolic hearts. Do they look remarkably different?\n\nProfessor: Yes, they do. Anatomical hearts are the actual organs in our bodies that pump blood, while symbolic hearts are the simplified, iconic representations we often see in art and popular culture. So, the correct answer would be A) True.\n\nStudent: Can you explain the main differences between the two?\n\nProfessor: Of course. An anatomical heart is a complex, muscular organ with chambers, valves, and blood vessels. It has a more irregular shape and is responsible for pumping blood throughout our bodies. On the other hand, a symbolic heart is a simple, symmetrical shape with two rounded lobes at the top and a pointed end at the bottom. It is often used to represent love and affection.\n\nStudent: I see. So, the symbolic heart is not an accurate representation of the actual heart?\n\nProfessor: That's correct. The symbolic heart is a highly stylized and simplified version of the anatomical heart. It does not accurately depict the structure or function of the actual organ.\n\nStudent: How did the symbolic heart become associated with love and emotions?\n\nProfessor: The association between the heart and emotions dates back to ancient civilizations, such as the Egyptians and Greeks, who believed that the heart was the center of emotions and intellect. Over time, this association evolved, and the heart became a symbol of love and affection in Western culture. The simplified, iconic heart shape we know today likely developed during the Middle Ages and has persisted ever since.\n\nStudent: Thank you, Professor. I now understand the difference between anatomical and symbolic hearts and how they came to be associated with love and emotions.",
