                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the nature of the Scarecrow from the Wizard of Oz and the causes of cerebral palsy.\n\nThe Scarecrow is a fictional character from the Wizard of Oz, created by L. Frank Baum. He is not a human being but rather a sentient scarecrow made of straw and cloth. Cerebral palsy, on the other hand, is a neurological disorder that affects muscle coordination and body movement. It is caused by damage to the developing brain, usually before, during, or shortly after birth.\n\nNow, let's generate sub-questions to help answer the original question:\n\n1. Can a non-human character like the Scarecrow develop human neurological disorders?\n2. Can a character made of straw and cloth have a brain that can be damaged?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n\n1. As a non-human character, the Scarecrow does not possess the same biological makeup as a human being. Therefore, it is unlikely that he could develop human neurological disorders like cerebral palsy.\n2. The Scarecrow is made of straw and cloth, which means he does not have a brain that can be damaged, leading to cerebral palsy.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, we can conclude that:\n\nB) False - A young Wizard of Oz Scarecrow could not have gotten cerebral palsy, as he is a non-human character made of straw and cloth, without a brain that can be damaged.",
                        "answers": [
