                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step-by-step procedure:\n1. Identify the role of gravity in the formation of stars.\n2. Evaluate each answer choice to determine which one best describes this role.\n\nGoing through each answer choice:\nA) Gravity converting solid matter into gases and light energy is not accurate. Gravity does not create energy, but rather it is a force that pulls matter together.\nB) Gravity causing gases and dust particles to condense into spheres is a correct description of the role of gravity in star formation. As gravity pulls matter together, it causes it to condense and form a sphere, which can eventually become a star.\nC) Gravity cooling gases and liquids until they become one solid mass is not accurate. Gravity does not cool matter, but rather it pulls it together.\nD) Gravity pushing rocks and dust particles outward from a dense center is not accurate. Gravity pulls matter inward towards a dense center, rather than pushing it outward.\n\nTherefore, the best answer is B) Gravity causes gases and dust particles to condense into spheres.",
