                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The gravitational pull of celestial bodies can affect the tides in the ocean.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What causes the tides in the ocean?\n2. How does the gravitational pull of celestial bodies affect the tides?\n3. Which celestial body has the greatest influence on the tides?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1: The tides in the ocean are caused by the gravitational pull of the Moon and the Sun.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2: The gravitational pull of the Moon and the Sun causes the ocean to bulge towards them, creating high tides. When the Moon and Sun are at right angles to each other, the gravitational pull is weaker and creates low tides.\n\nAnswering sub-question 3: The Moon has the greatest influence on the tides because it is closer to the Earth than the Sun. The gravitational pull of the Moon is about twice as strong as the gravitational pull of the Sun on the Earth's tides.\n\nAnswering the original question: The differences in water level at the beach are primarily due to the gravitational influence of the Moon. Therefore, the answer is B) the Moon.",
