                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking about the primary cause of the differences in water levels at the beach, which is related to the phenomenon of tides. Tides are the rise and fall of sea levels caused by the combined effects of the gravitational forces exerted by the Moon, the Sun, and the rotation of the Earth.\n\nAdditional facts:\n- The gravitational force of the Moon is stronger than that of the Sun due to its closer proximity to the Earth.\n- The gravitational force of asteroids and comets is negligible compared to the Moon and the Sun.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) What causes tides?\n2) Which celestial body has the strongest gravitational influence on tides?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) Tides are caused by the combined effects of the gravitational forces exerted by the Moon, the Sun, and the rotation of the Earth.\n2) The Moon has the strongest gravitational influence on tides due to its closer proximity to the Earth.\n\nAnswer to the original question: The differences in water levels at the beach that Miriam notices are primarily due to the gravitational influence of the Moon (option B).",
                        "answers": [
