                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain the process of surface rock formation on volcanoes?\n\nProfessor: Sure. The major process of surface rock formation on volcanoes is when rock cools quickly from melted rock.\n\nStudent: How does the rock cool quickly?\n\nProfessor: When magma or lava flows out of a volcano, it is exposed to the cooler air and rapidly cools down, forming solid rock.\n\nStudent: So, the rock is formed from melted rock?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. The melted rock, or magma, cools and solidifies to form the rock.\n\nStudent: Is this process the same for all types of volcanoes?\n\nProfessor: No, it can vary depending on the type of volcano. For example, shield volcanoes have a more fluid lava that can flow for long distances before cooling, while stratovolcanoes have a more viscous lava that cools and solidifies more quickly.\n\nStudent: I see. So, the type of volcano affects the process of surface rock formation?\n\nProfessor: Exactly. The type of volcano and the characteristics of its lava can affect how quickly the rock cools and solidifies.",
