                    {
                        "id": "97e0ad26-469b-4298-bb62-fddcf296e292",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe major process of surface rock formation on volcanoes is A) Rock cools quickly from melted rock. This is because when a volcano erupts, molten rock (magma) is expelled from the volcano and quickly cools and solidifies as it is exposed to the air. This process is known as extrusive igneous rock formation. The molten rock is cooled so quickly that it does not have time to crystallize, resulting in a fine-grained texture. This is in contrast to intrusive igneous rock formation, which occurs when molten rock solidifies slowly deep underground, allowing it to crystallize and form a coarse-grained texture. Rock is not formed from deposited sediment, nor is it recrystallized by extreme pressure.",
                        "answers": [
