                    {
                        "id": "71c0caf9-7dc3-4200-a2b8-2e3b8ecb4cd1",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: I'm not sure about the next step in the formation of sedimentary rocks after a layer of sediment is deposited on the ocean floor. Can you help me understand the process better?\n\nProfessor: Of course! The process of forming sedimentary rocks involves several steps. After the deposition of sediments on the ocean floor, the next step is compaction and cementation, which is essentially the burying of the sediments. So the correct answer would be B) burying.\n\nStudent: I see. But how does the compaction and cementation process work?\n\nProfessor: Good question! Compaction occurs when the weight of the overlying sediments squeezes the particles in the lower layers closer together. This reduces the space between the particles and forces out any water that may be present.\n\nStudent: And what about cementation?\n\nProfessor: Cementation is the process by which dissolved minerals, like silica or calcium carbonate, precipitate out of the water and fill the spaces between the sediment particles. These minerals act as a glue, binding the particles together and forming a solid rock.\n\nStudent: So, after the sediments are compacted and cemented, do we have a sedimentary rock?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct! Once the sediments have been compacted and cemented, they form a sedimentary rock. There are various types of sedimentary rocks, such as sandstone, limestone, and shale, depending on the composition of the sediments and the cementing material.\n\nStudent: I understand now. So the answer is B) burying because that's when the compaction and cementation processes occur, which ultimately lead to the formation of sedimentary rocks.\n\nProfessor: Exactly! You've got it. Do you have any other questions about sedimentary rocks or the formation process?\n\nStudent: No, I think I understand it now. Thank you for your help!\n\nProfessor: You're welcome! If you have any more questions in the future, don't hesitate to ask.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "71e7071e-be90-4ffc-8c39-09f51d740b0d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) burying.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
