                    {
                        "id": "e17904af-f459-4532-9f97-df3b0f75516f",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe question asks us to identify the effect that extensive glaciation could have had on ocean ecosystems at the end of the Permian period, which may have contributed to extinctions. We need to use reasoning to determine which of the given options is the most likely effect.\n\nOption A suggests that glaciation could have caused a steep drop in ocean salinity and density. This is possible because as glaciers form on land, they remove water from the ocean, which could increase salinity and decrease density. However, it is not clear how this would disrupt ocean ecosystems or contribute to extinctions.\n\nOption B suggests that glaciation could have caused a steep drop in ocean volume and area. This is also possible because as glaciers form on land, they lock up water that would otherwise be in the ocean, which could decrease the overall volume and area of the ocean. However, it is not clear how this would disrupt ocean ecosystems or contribute to extinctions.\n\nOption C suggests that glaciation could have increased exposure of oceans to ultraviolet radiation. This is possible because as glaciers form on land, they reflect more sunlight back into space, which could decrease the amount of atmospheric ozone that absorbs ultraviolet radiation. This could increase the amount of ultraviolet radiation that reaches the ocean surface, which could be harmful to some ocean organisms. This is a plausible explanation for how glaciation could have disrupted ocean ecosystems and contributed to extinctions.\n\nOption D suggests that glaciation could have increased exposure of tsunamis to ocean life. This is unlikely because tsunamis are typically caused by earthquakes or volcanic eruptions, not glaciation. It is not clear how this would be related to the extinctions at the end of the Permian period.\n\nTherefore, the most likely effect of extensive glaciation on ocean ecosystems at the end of the Permian period is increased exposure of oceans to ultraviolet radiation, which is described in option C.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "fcaf57be-6aa2-4af0-9d8b-ace8ac4056fd",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) increased exposure of oceans to ultraviolet radiation.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
