                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question: What effect could extensive glaciation, which has been proposed as a cause for extinctions at the end of the Permian period, have had on ocean ecosystems?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step:\n\n1. Extensive glaciation means that a large amount of water would be frozen and stored in glaciers.\n2. This would lead to a decrease in the amount of liquid water in the oceans.\n3. As a result, the concentration of salt in the remaining ocean water would increase, causing a rise in ocean salinity and density (opposite of option A).\n4. The decrease in liquid water could also lead to a reduction in ocean volume and area (option B).\n5. With less water in the oceans, there might be an increased exposure to ultraviolet radiation (option C), but this is not directly related to glaciation.\n6. Tsunamis are caused by underwater earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, or landslides, and their exposure to ocean life is not directly related to glaciation (option D).\n\nBased on our reasoning, the correct answer is B) steep drop in ocean volume and area.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1b4a63d9-6b06-406c-a7b7-65c9e6147c15",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) steep drop in ocean volume and area.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
