                        "cot": "\n\nStudent: I don't understand how we can know there was glaciation if we don't have any direct evidence. \nProfessor: There are a few ways that we can infer that there was glaciation. For example, looking at the geologic record, we can see that there was a sudden drop in temperature around the time of the Permian extinction. This could be due to a number of factors, but one possibility is that there was widespread glaciation. \nStudent: But how would that produce the extinction? \nProfessor: Well, there are a few ways that it could have. For example, if there was a sudden drop in ocean temperature, that could have disrupted the ocean ecosystem, which could have had a ripple effect and led to the extinction of many species. \nStudent: I see. So it's not that the glaciation directly caused the extinction, but it created conditions that were conducive to extinction. \nProfessor: Exactly.",
