                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What weather event is the most probable cause of a forest fire?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Forest fires are caused by a combination of dry weather conditions and a spark or ignition source.\n- A flash flood is unlikely to cause a forest fire because it involves heavy rain and flooding, which would make the forest wet and less prone to fire.\n- A tornado is also unlikely to cause a forest fire because it involves strong winds and may even bring rain, which would have a similar effect as a flash flood.\n- A hailstorm may cause damage to trees and vegetation, but it is not likely to start a fire.\n- A lightning strike, on the other hand, is a common cause of forest fires because it can ignite dry vegetation and start a fire that can quickly spread.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) a lightning strike.",
