                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Recall that when light hits a surface, it can be either absorbed, transmitted, refracted, or reflected.\n\nStep 2: Consider a mirror, which is a smooth and shiny surface made of a highly reflective material such as glass with a thin layer of metal on the back.\n\nStep 3: When light hits a mirror, it is reflected back in a predictable way, following the law of reflection. This means that the angle of incidence (the angle between the incoming light ray and the normal to the surface) is equal to the angle of reflection (the angle between the reflected light ray and the normal).\n\nStep 4: Therefore, we can conclude that the correct answer is B) reflected. Most of the light that hits a mirror is reflected back, rather than being absorbed, transmitted, or refracted.",
                        "answers": [
