                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, let's first understand the meaning of each option:\n\nA) Refracted: Refraction is the bending of light as it passes from one medium to another (e.g., from air to water). This is not what happens when light hits a mirror.\n\nB) Reflected: Reflection is the bouncing of light off a surface. This is what mirrors are designed to do - they have a smooth, shiny surface that allows light to bounce off and create a reflection.\n\nC) Absorbed: Absorption is when light is taken in by a material and converted into heat or another form of energy. While some light may be absorbed by a mirror, this is not the primary interaction between light and a mirror.\n\nD) Transmitted: Transmission is when light passes through a material. Mirrors are not transparent, so light does not pass through them.\n\nNow that we understand the meanings of each option, we can determine the correct answer.\n\nWhen light hits a mirror, most of the light is:\n\nB) reflected.\n\nMirrors are designed to reflect light, creating a reflection of the objects in front of them. While some light may be absorbed or refracted, the primary interaction between light and a mirror is reflection.",
