                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "B) Amplitude\n\nCritique: The pitch of a sound wave is determined by its frequency, which is the number of cycles per second. Therefore, if both sound waves have the same pitch, their frequencies must be the same. Wavelength is the distance between two consecutive peaks or troughs of a sound wave, and it is related to frequency and speed by the equation wavelength = speed/frequency. Since the pitch is the same for both sound waves, their frequencies are the same, and therefore their wavelengths must also be the same if they are traveling at the same speed in air. The speed of sound in air is a constant value, so it cannot be greater for the second sound wave. Therefore, the only property that can be greater for the second sound wave is its amplitude, which is the maximum displacement of air particles from their rest position. \n\nFinal answer: B) Amplitude",
